2. A number $x$ is continually $\epsilon$-adherent to $(a_n)$ if for every $N$, the number $x$ is $\epsilon$-adherent to the tail sequence $(a_n)_{n=N}^{\infty}$, which means that for every $N$ there is some $n \geq N$ such that $d(x, a_n) < \epsilon$.

Now, suppose that $x$ is eventually $\epsilon$-close to the sequence $(a_n)$. Let the corresponding index be $N_0$, meaning for all $n \geq N_0$, the distance $d(x, a_n) < \epsilon$.

If we are to examine whether $x$ is continually $\epsilon$-adherent to the sequence, we examine the condition for all $N$:

The tail of the sequence starting at $N$ is by definition the sequence from $a_N$ onwards. Since $x$ is eventually $\epsilon$-close to the sequence, for any fixed $N$ (which is less than or equal to $N_0$), all terms from the $N_0$-th term onwards are within $\epsilon$ of our number $x$. Therefore, for $N \leq N_0$, the sequence from $N$ onwards includes all these terms, and thus the condition is satisfied.

However, if $N > N_0$, then again, terms from $N_0$ onwards are those we have considered in the first case. And fundamentally, all elements from the $N_0$-th term onwards remain within the stipulated distance. Since for some $n \geq N$ (where $N \leq N_0$ or $n\geq N > N_0$), the distance between $a_n$ and $x$ is less than $\epsilon$, especially for $n > N_0$.

Thus, in both scenarios (when $N \leq N_0$ and $N > N_0$), it is evident that for all $N$, we can always find terms of the sequence (starting from some point along from $N$) which are within $\epsilon$ distance from $x$.

Therefore, if x is eventually $\epsilon$-close to the sequence, with some $N$ such that the distance from $x$ to any term of the sequence from index $N$ onwards is less than $\epsilon$, then $x$ is also continually $\epsilon$-adherent. 

